{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_22-44-04-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nd={\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Completed the citation trail: two retained receipts were still uncited. 99718ab469974eb1ad40516efe7c999e is the first harness run whose CA_EVENT lines were rejected for lacking an event-name field, and 32cd246ad3824df0b808f51ef1d36182 is the live-stall run whose admission pinned exact fixture indexes that the schedule shifted, leaving every comparison unknown. Both are failures of observation or specification rather than completed comparisons, and both are now cited in the accounts that describe them, with their conclusions, defect judgments and limits unchanged.\\\",\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"e99a162d27c7421fa6729d8cb9703581-question-0\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-scan\\\",\\\"s-restorecommitted\\\",\\\"s-processlogs\\\",\\\"s-confentry\\\",\\\"s-quorumsize\\\",\\\"s-votegrant\\\",\\\"s-doc\\\",\\\"s-cfgdoc\\\",\\\"s-staging\\\",\\\"s-dupskip\\\",\\\"s-installsnap\\\",\\\"s-voteuptodate\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"99718ab469974eb1ad40516efe7c999e\\\":[0,1,2,3,4,5],\n    \\\"f4adc58c4125434eb91cacf438a6da50\\\":[3,1,5,0,2,4],\n    \\\"b68f3d3a08b34cd6a27b76faeb0d2a3e\\\":[2,1,0],\n    \\\"455e647ad0794a16903414b5a8846276\\\":[3,2,1,0],\n    \\\"fa1f90c2eefd45e6af45d7593ac53205\\\":[3],\n    \\\"112186e8b9544b51b4f7818a04c78a84\\\":[4],\n    \\\"531b0a48500843228e26623ce7333943\\\":[1,6],\n    \\\"861a2b3c62bb44d7aed2eee9b95aa15f\\\":[3,8],\n    \\\"182740a6d29f4af9a99cf83c49f2e857\\\":[4,10],\n    \\\"6ff0ef691a0141449142bfa06719bb47\\\":[5]},\n   \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Ten receipts of two experiment families over one defect chain. 99718ab469974eb1ad40516efe7c999e is retained as a channel failure: the harness emitted its events without an event-name field, so the runner recorded six invalid observations and no comparison was evaluated; nothing is claimed from it. The remaining nine receipts share the structural fixture (snapshot configuration {A,B,C}, a committed configuration entry with {A,B,C,D,E} above the snapshot, a persisted commit index covering it) and the feature-on restart reporting {A,B,C} against the control reporting {A,B,C,D,E}. No value is combined across receipts; each comparison outcome is read from its own receipt, and each later revision restricted its comparison set to its new predicates.\\\",\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"explained\\\",\\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Current account: with Config.RestoreCommittedLogs enabled and a CommitTrackingLogStore whose persisted commit index covers a committed configuration entry newer than the node's last snapshot, NewRaft never rebuilds the node's configuration from that entry, so the restarted node keeps the snapshot's membership for candidate admission and election quorum; that stale view lets it lead with two votes where the current configuration requires three, produces two concurrent leaders whose commits collide at the same index and term, and leaves the entry and applied state different across the cluster until the node's state is replaced. The first harness run for this question (99718ab469974eb1ad40516efe7c999e) is retained as a channel failure: its events carried no event-name field, so six observations were rejected and no comparison was evaluated; it establishes nothing and is not used. Basis: restoreFromCommittedLogs reads the persisted commit index and calls processLogs(commitIndex, nil) (api.go 724-753); processLogs advances lastApplied (raft.go 1361-1362); the configuration scan starts at max(lastSnapshotIndex, lastApplied)+1 (api.go 610-620) above the committed entry, whereas a restart without the replay scans from the snapshot index and processes it (receipt 531b0a48500843228e26623ce7333943 index 6) and a later configuration entry is processed normally (receipt 182740a6d29f4af9a99cf83c49f2e857 index 4); quorum and voter admission come from configurations.latest (raft.go 313-320, 1085-1096, 1660-1680); duplicates are skipped so neither the configuration entry the restart missed nor the conflicting command is reprocessed (raft.go 1526-1553); the persisted commit index exists only because the node ran with the feature enabled (raft.go 1393-1403); and an installed snapshot replaces log, FSM and configuration (raft.go 1935-1972). Remaining limits: the membership change was a single harness-issued AddVoter; the long run is entry-count based and contains no snapshot install; the per-node repair needed harness-arranged lag and the restore used a self-produced snapshot; ordinary log comparison is argued unable to reconcile the divergence from the vote rule rather than tested; and in-memory stores with scaled timeouts and a graceful shutdown were used.\\\",\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"The configuration change is a single harness-issued AddVoter and the long run is ~200 entries without a snapshot install.\\\",\n    \\\"The per-node repair needed harness-arranged lag and the cluster-wide repair used a snapshot the leader produced itself.\\\",\n    \\\"Ordinary log comparison is argued unable to reconcile the divergence from the vote rule rather than tested.\\\",\n    \\\"In-memory stores, scaled timeouts and a graceful shutdown rather than a crash.\\\",\n    \\\"The first harness receipt for this question is a channel failure that establishes nothing.\\\"],\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"The feature-off restart control over the identical cluster scenario, long run, configuration change and restore produces no stale membership, no minority leader and no self-applied write.\\\",\n    \\\"A later configuration entry ends the stale membership, and both replacement paths remove the divergent entry and applied state.\\\",\n    \\\"Without a replacement the earlier receipts show the divergence persisting while the node keeps up, so the consequences are bounded by repair timing rather than by ordinary operation.\\\"]},\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"e99a162d27c7421fa6729d8cb9703581-question-1\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-restorecommitted\\\",\\\"s-processlogs\\\",\\\"s-fsmch\\\",\\\"s-startup-order\\\",\\\"s-snapfail\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193\\\":[1,3,2,0],\n    \\\"e0d98aadc54b461792883fc8a2a82676\\\":[1,3,2,0],\n    \\\"ea93eea3cc08408ab3cd208ca9b1e5ca\\\":[1,3,2,0],\n    \\\"00386ad8ea534d91a0ef7506fabc9527\\\":[3,7,1,5,2,4],\n    \\\"32cd246ad3824df0b808f51ef1d36182\\\":[0,1,2,3],\n    \\\"2d92d633d32a4a7c9e204b7dc5c8828b\\\":[0,1,2,3]},\n   \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Six receipts over one question and one mechanism. 2d92d633d32a4a7c9e204b7dc5c8828b is retained as an identity failure (construct events without local_id, no evaluated comparison) and 32cd246ad3824df0b808f51ef1d36182 as an over-specified-admission failure (the admission pinned snapshot and commit indexes that the schedule shifted, so no comparison was evaluated); neither establishes a comparison outcome, though the second's raw events showed the intended behaviour. 00386ad8ea534d91a0ef7506fabc9527 witnesses the stall on harness-supplied stores at two scales with feature-off controls. ea93eea3cc08408ab3cd208ca9b1e5ca reaches it in a live cluster with MaxAppendEntries 1; e0d98aadc54b461792883fc8a2a82676 repeats that with the default 64 and about 8300 entries while snapshots were delayed; 49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193 repeats it with the default 64 and a snapshot store whose every attempt failed and was tolerated. Each comparison outcome is read from its own receipt.\\\",\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"explained\\\",\\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Answer: yes, and the trigger is operationally realistic rather than harness-arranged. Snapshot failures are logged and tolerated by the snapshot loop (snapshot.go runSnapshots; receipt 49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193 index 0 records four tolerated failures), so a node whose snapshot store keeps failing keeps its last successful snapshot while its persisted commit index advances; when the accumulated entries exceed 128*MaxAppendEntries the restart cannot finish, because restoreFromCommittedLogs replays them through processLogs into fsmMutateCh before NewRaft starts the only consumer runFSM. At the default batch size the requirement is 8193 entries, just above the default SnapshotThreshold, so a node needs failing or delayed snapshots to reach it - both are now observed (receipt e0d98aadc54b461792883fc8a2a82676 for delay, 49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193 for failure), with the supplied-store receipts 00386ad8ea534d91a0ef7506fabc9527 giving the two-scale controls. Two earlier receipts are retained as failures that establish no comparison: 32cd246ad3824df0b808f51ef1d36182 whose admission pinned exact fixture indexes that the schedule shifted, and 2d92d633d32a4a7c9e204b7dc5c8828b whose construct events omitted the identity field. Remaining limits: the failing snapshot store is a harness wrapper and only Create failures are injected; non-return is observed over a bounded window with permanence argued from source; stores are in-memory, the shutdown graceful and no supervision, timeout or restart policy is modelled.\\\",\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"The failing snapshot store is a harness wrapper and only Create failures are injected; a production store's failure modes or a disk-full style failure are not covered.\\\",\n    \\\"Non-return is observed over a bounded 5s window in each receipt; permanence rests on the source argument about the missing consumer.\\\",\n    \\\"In-memory stores and a graceful shutdown; the stalled constructor is abandoned rather than resumed and no supervision, timeout or restart policy is modelled.\\\",\n    \\\"Two earlier receipts for this question are retained as failures that establish no comparison outcome.\\\"],\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"Feature-off controls over the identical stores construct normally in every completed receipt, so the gap and the stores alone do not explain the stall.\\\",\n    \\\"No-op entries never reach the FSM commit path and do not stall construction, which scopes the defect to command or barrier entries.\\\",\n    \\\"In the live receipts the other voters kept a quorum, so the defect is a node-level startup failure rather than a cluster-wide outage at that size.\\\"]}]}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('written')\nPY\ncd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_22-44-04-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_22-44-04-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
